 ## Background
-The use of tetrahedra in volume rendering originated from the need to handle unstructured grids and resolve topological ambiguities in isosurface extraction. The Marching Tetrahedrons Algorithm was developed to overcome the ambiguities of Marching Cubes by ensuring consistent topology [S1, unofficial]. Projected Tetrahedra was introduced as an object-order rendering technique to leverage hardware rasterization for unstructured data [S1, S2, unofficial][S3][S4, S6, S7][S8, S9, S10, unofficial][S11][S12, unofficial][S14, S16][S17, S18, unofficial]. Parallel methods using kd-trees and grid-based traversal were later developed to handle large-scale datasets and improve performance [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S8767]. The technique is used in scientific visualization, medical imaging, and differentiable rendering frameworks [S1, unofficial][S4].
+The tetrahedral representation for volume rendering originated from the need to handle unstructured grids and curvilinear volumes where standard hexahedral cell projection suffers from non-planar faces and ambiguous ray intersections [S4]. The decomposition of cubes into tetrahedra (typically 5 or 6) was established to resolve these topological issues [S2, S8, S13, S19, unofficial][S1, unofficial]. The Projected Tetrahedra method was introduced to leverage graphics hardware acceleration by rasterizing projected tetrahedra [S1, S2, unofficial][S3][S4, S6, S7][S8, S9, S10, unofficial][S11][S12, unofficial][S14, S16][S17, S18, unofficial]. Subsequent developments focused on parallelization [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial], bandwidth optimization via strips and fans [S1, S2, unofficial], and integration with differentiable rendering frameworks [S1, unofficial][S4, S5].
 
